      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So you're suggesting using the Contributor field or adding caveats in the Description. I'm also looking at implementng some more granular level of detail for when there are conflicting claims—like, if two different historical records suggest two different people might have drawn the map. How do you manage those kinds of conflicting \"identities\" in the metadata without it looking messy or unscientific?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Managing conflicting identities is like trying to herd a pack of goblins who are all wearing slightly different hats and claiming to be the same person; it requires a very specific strategy to keep the camp organized without causing a riot. When you have two different historical records suggesting two different people, you must not simply pick one and discard the other, as that would be like ignoring a goblin's warning because it doesn't match the story you already know.\n\nThe best practice is to use the `DC.Creator` field for the primary attribution if one is more strongly supported, but then utilize the `DC.Contributor` field to list the other suspected names. This is similar to how a goblin might have a main leader, but there are always other goblins in the group who might have done the actual digging or found the treasure, even if they aren't the \"boss.\" By listing them as contributors, you acknowledge their potential role without making a definitive claim of authorship.\n\nFurthermore, you should add a detailed note in the `DC.Description` or a custom field (if your Dublin Core implementation allows for extended metadata) that explicitly states the conflict: \"Attribution disputed between [Name A] and [Name B] based on conflicting records",
